<ResourceDictionary xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
                    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml">

    <!--
        How big the gap is - between one thing and the next, and between a thing and its own edge.
        Every margin and every padding this window draws, and nothing else: not one size, not one
        shape, not one colour, and not one style.

        THE FIFTH VALUE FILE, CUT OUT OF Values.xaml ON 2026-09-15, AND THE MARKUP RATCHET IS WHAT
        ASKED - for the fourth time on that file and the fourth time pointing at a seam its own
        opening sentence had already named. Values.xaml stood exactly on the ceiling of 376 lines,
        and the plan sheet needed three widths at once: the box that takes a number of seconds (which
        stood in the view as a raw 56 - backlog 352), the sheet itself (a raw 560), and a ceiling on
        the button that now names the entry it acts on. A value file has to hold them, the guard
        allows exactly the value files it names, and none of the other three answers "how wide is
        that box". Backlog 173, second of its three roads, owner's decision.

        THE SEAM PASSES THE TEST EVERY EARLIER ONE HAD TO: a name that says what is inside without
        opening the file. Colours.xaml answers "what colour", Columns.xaml "how wide is that
        column", Type.xaml "how big is that letter", Values.xaml "how big is that thing and what
        shape is that mark" - and this file answers "how much air is around it". Twenty-one entries
        came here, every Margin and every Pad, each with the comment it had. The four Thickness
        entries that are NOT gaps stayed behind on purpose: a border thickness and a focus ring
        thickness say how thick a LINE is, which is a shape question, and Values.xaml keeps those.

        MERGED RIGHT AFTER Values.xaml AND BEFORE ANY STYLE FILE, which is load bearing in the
        ordinary way: every style file reaches these names through StaticResource while it is being
        read, so this dictionary has to exist before the first of them is opened. Nothing here reads
        a name from anywhere else, so its own position among the value files is free.

        ADR-23 applies here as it does to the rest: a margin written into a view instead of named
        here fails the build, and the reason is not tidiness. XAML is prose, a gap of seven instead
        of eight breaks nothing, and that is exactly how an interface drifts into looking like two
        products.
    -->

    <!--
        A SPACING SCALE OF FOUR STEPS STOOD IN Values.xaml UNTIL 2026-09-02 AND NOTHING HAD EVER
        USED IT. SpaceTight, SpaceNormal, SpaceWide and SpaceSection - zero references in markup,
        zero in code. Its own comment said "the guard makes the alternative fail the build", and
        there was no such guard: every margin in this window is a named Thickness instead, written
        one at a time. That sentence is the exact shape rule 3 warns about - a "because" nothing
        checks, read by later sessions as a fact. Backlog 294 keeps the intent, which was a good one.
    -->
    <Thickness x:Key="PadCell">8,4,8,4</Thickness>

    <!--
        The list cell, and it is deliberately not PadCell.

        Measured on screen 2026-08-02: WPF UI's cell template honours TemplateBinding Padding
        and the built-in one did not, so the same 8,4,8,4 that was invisible before the
        migration became 6.6 logical pixels of row height after it - 29 rows on screen fell to
        22. The owner chose to keep this list's density, so the vertical half goes to nothing
        and row height is decided by the line height alone.
    -->
    <Thickness x:Key="PadListCell">8,0,8,0</Thickness>
    <Thickness x:Key="PadPanel">16</Thickness>
    <Thickness x:Key="MarginBetweenControls">0,0,8,0</Thickness>
    <Thickness x:Key="MarginNone">0</Thickness>

    <!-- The gap between a mark and its word. The mark itself is ShapeMark, in Values.xaml. -->
    <Thickness x:Key="MarginAfterShape">0,0,8,0</Thickness>

    <!--
        THE GAP BETWEEN A NUMBER AND THE WORDS IT COUNTS, AND IT WAS MISSING UNTIL 2026-09-01.
        The overview - the first screen anybody sees - read "113services running", "1set to start
        automatically and did not come up", "0orphans". Every line of it.

        WHY IT WAS MISSING IS WORTH MORE THAN THE VALUE. The label carried MarginBetweenControls,
        which is 0,0,8,0 - a gap on its RIGHT, where nothing stands - so it pushed away from what
        came after it and left nothing between itself and the number in front. A margin named for
        what it separates rather than for which side it is on reads as though it does both.

        FOUND BY LOOKING AT THE SCREEN. Backlog 192's package asked for the first run screen to be
        seen by an eye rather than counted by automation, and this is what the eye was for: every
        guard in this project passed over it, because a missing space is not a missing element.
    -->
    <Thickness x:Key="MarginAfterNumber">0,0,8,0</Thickness>

    <!--
        THE GAP BETWEEN WHAT LEADS A SUGGESTION ROW AND WHAT FOLLOWS IT, 2026-09-16. There was none:
        the row borrowed MarginAfterNumber, which is 8 on the RIGHT of the sentence, so on the
        owner's photograph "status:running" and "Switched off and running anyway" read as one word.
    -->
    <Thickness x:Key="MarginSuggestionTrail">16,0,0,0</Thickness>

    <Thickness x:Key="MarginAboveStatus">0,8,0,0</Thickness>
    <Thickness x:Key="MarginBelowSearch">0,0,0,8</Thickness>
    <!--
        The sentence under a field: a small gap from the field above it, and the same right edge
        as the field, which stops MarginBetweenControls short of the column beside it. 2026-09-23,
        the line under the search box (UX-GUI-002).
    -->
    <Thickness x:Key="MarginUnderField">0,4,8,0</Thickness>

    <!--
        Between one filter chip and the next, and it is the only new appearance value the whole
        clickable-filter slice needed.

        Right and bottom rather than all four sides, because the chips wrap: a gap on the leading
        edge would push the first chip of every line away from the search box above it, and the
        eye reads that as an indent rather than as spacing. The bottom half is what keeps two
        wrapped lines apart.
    -->
    <Thickness x:Key="MarginChip">0,0,8,4</Thickness>

    <!--
        WHAT MAKES A CHIP LOOK LIKE A CHIP AND NOT LIKE A BUTTON - 2026-09-01, and the case for it
        is a measurement rather than taste. Read off the automation tree with the window at
        1650x1050: a filter chip was 47 pixels tall and an action button beside it was 48. Two
        controls that do completely different things - one writes a token into the query, the other
        opens a plan that can stop a service - were drawn as the same rounded rectangle at the same
        height with the same text size. `docs/11` complaint 14 is exactly this fault one control
        over, and its rule is the one being followed here: when the look carries the wrong MEANING,
        the repair is a whole visual family rather than a different shade of the old one.

        THREE SIGNALS RATHER THAN ONE, because any single one of them is arguable and three
        together are not: the corner becomes a pill (CornerChip, in Values.xaml), the height drops
        by about a fifth, and the text drops to the body size. A person does not have to notice any
        of them to stop confusing the two rows.

        AND IT PAYS FOR ITSELF IN THE ONE CURRENCY THIS WINDOW IS SHORT OF. Measured the same run:
        517 of 1050 pixels stand above the first row of data, and the four rows of chips are 206 of
        those. This takes about 36 back, which is a row of services - small, and in the right
        direction, which is more than the previous three attempts at this screen managed.
    -->
    <Thickness x:Key="PadChip">12,3,12,3</Thickness>

    <!--
        Inside the one button this product draws itself. It WAS 14,6 so the box would land on the
        library button's 48 device pixels, and the owner asked on 2026-09-01 for the elevate button
        to be smaller. Matching the neighbours' box was buying nothing here: a primary action is
        told apart by its fill, and this one never stands beside them anyway.
    -->
    <Thickness x:Key="PadButton">10,4,10,4</Thickness>

    <!--
        Grouping, in the filter row and in the column picker. The gap between facets is wider than
        the gap between chips on purpose - that difference is the only thing on screen saying where
        one question ends. The picker's ceiling - HeightMenu, in Values.xaml - is what makes it
        scroll instead of running off the bottom of a short screen and losing its last items
        silently.
    -->
    <Thickness x:Key="MarginGroup">0,0,20,0</Thickness>

    <!--
        THE GAP ABOVE A SECTION HEADING, 2026-08-19, and it exists because MarginGroup has NO
        VERTICAL COMPONENT AT ALL - it is 0,0,20,0, a right margin. Every heading in the plan
        panel used it and every one of them therefore sat flush against the sentence above,
        which is what the owner saw as one wall of text with no hierarchy.

        Bottom is small on purpose: a heading belongs to what is under it, so the space above it
        has to be larger than the space below or the eye groups it with the wrong block.
    -->
    <Thickness x:Key="MarginSection">0,18,0,4</Thickness>

    <!--
        Inside the plan panel, between its edge and its text. Without it the first character sits
        on the line separating the panel from the list, which is what made the two read as one
        surface however the fill was chosen.
    -->
    <Thickness x:Key="PaddingPanel">14,12,14,12</Thickness>

    <!-- Between the rule above the panel's footer - BorderFooterRule, in Values.xaml - and the button under it. -->
    <Thickness x:Key="PaddingFooter">0,10,0,0</Thickness>

    <!--
        THE MACHINE OVERVIEW AS THREE CARDS RATHER THAN SIX LINES, 2026-09-01. Why the composition
        changed instead of the colours is at the head of OverviewView.xaml, beside the screen it is
        about - these are the gaps that drawing needs, and the ones that are not reused say why.

        EIGHT DOWN EACH SIDE RATHER THAN SIXTEEN, FROM 2026-09-01 EVENING, and the other eight
        moved onto the buttons inside. The card looks identical - a line still starts sixteen from
        its edge - and what changes is that the hover surface of a question now has air around the
        words instead of ending on the first letter. Photographed: the pointer lit a panel whose
        left boundary ran through the "1".
    -->
    <Thickness x:Key="PadTile">8,14,8,16</Thickness>

    <!--
        Around the plan sheet, which floats over a dimmed window rather than sitting in a row. The
        margin is also the sheet's height limit - centred inside it, it cannot outgrow the window.
        `docs/11` 3.12. Its edge, corner and width are in Values.xaml, under BorderAllSides.
    -->
    <Thickness x:Key="MarginPlanSheet">40</Thickness>

    <!--
        Between one card and the next. On the RIGHT ONLY, which leaves twelve points hanging off the
        last card of the row - accepted rather than answered with a negative margin on the row, which
        would be a value nothing on screen explains and a second thing to get wrong.
    -->
    <Thickness x:Key="MarginTile">0,0,12,0</Thickness>

    <!-- Between a card's number and its words, and above each block that follows the cards. -->
    <Thickness x:Key="MarginTileLabel">0,2,0,0</Thickness>
    <Thickness x:Key="MarginTileParts">0,14,0,0</Thickness>

    <!--
        UNDER EACH LINE OF THE DETAILS PANEL. Until 2026-09-16 the lines stood on one another with
        no air at all - twenty-eight device pixels per line at 150%, the pitch of a list that is
        scanned - and this panel is a form that is read. Six points, which is less than a row of
        the list and more than nothing, so a wrapped path still reads as one line of the form.
    -->
    <Thickness x:Key="MarginDetailLine">0,0,0,6</Thickness>

    <!--
        Around a mark in the details panel: the eight to the right that every mark in the list
        carries (MarginAfterShape), and five above so an eight-point mark sits on the first line of
        a value that may wrap to several - centred on the whole block it would float beside the
        third line of a path.
    -->
    <Thickness x:Key="MarginDetailMark">0,5,8,0</Thickness>
</ResourceDictionary>
